vice named inventory-worker on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"The unit /etc/systemd/system/inventory-worker.service was created with a continuous loop script (sleep 10 between ticks) and Restart=always, then enabled and started. systemctl status confirms it is loaded/enabled/active, and state.log shows repeated timestamped ticks proving continuous background operation both before and after a full node reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-52472e87303b4256\",\"cmd-dae0107326ed4c18\",\"cmd-7f3f7a1d845e4377\",\"cmd-e132fc40a11543ab\",\"cmd-9c82f14698c34324\"]},{\"id\":\"R2\",\"requirement\":\"Service is confined so it writes only inside /var/lib/inventory\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Using ReadOnlyDirectories=/ and ReadWriteDirectories=/var/lib/inventory (the systemd-219-era sandboxing directives, since ReadWritePaths/ProtectSystem=strict are unavailable on this systemd version), the service's mount namespace shows root mounted read-only with /var/lib/inventory as the sole read-write bind mount. Live tests as root inside the sandbox's mount namespace confirm writes outside /var/lib/inventory fail with 'Read-only file system' while writes inside succeed. This was re-verified identically on the new process after a full reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3279402c2cc54c81\",\"cmd-b3d1fdf8eb8f4477\",\"cmd-58aafb50912043f1\"]},{\"id\":\"R3\",\"requirement\":\"Service gains no new privileges\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"NoNewPrivileges=yes is set in the unit and /proc/<pid>/status shows NoNewPrivs: 1 for both the original and the post-reboot service process.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d687233642124853\",\"cmd-58aafb50912043f1\"]}],\"confidence\":0.93,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.95,\"summary\":\"All state-changing executor commands map cleanly to the task: creating the inventory-worker user/directory, the worker script, and the systemd unit are all required deliverables. Several verification steps created small test files outside and 